WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:18.851
Hi, my name is Alfredo Quiñones-Hinojosa.

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:23.422
I am an associate professor of neurosurgery, oncology,

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:27.627
cellular and molecular medicine in neuroscience at Johns Hopkins University

00:00:27.628 --> 00:00:31.163
in the Department of Neurosurgery and Oncology.

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:34.000
I am delighted to be here with you today

00:00:34.001 --> 00:00:37.270
and tell you a little bit of my story about

00:00:37.271 --> 00:00:42.842
how I became a physician scientist, which is what I do today.

00:00:42.843 --> 00:00:46.078
I show these three different pictures just to illustrate

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:54.020
some of my trajectory when I was that small young child growing up in Mexico.

00:00:54.021 --> 00:00:58.658
As you can actually see, some of my friends have actually teased me

00:00:58.659 --> 00:01:01.594
over time saying that I really do have a big head,

00:01:01.595 --> 00:01:08.067
and the reality is that I had a big head all the way back to when I was a little boy.

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:11.938
And it's not hydrocephalus, which is accumulation of water on the brain.

00:01:11.939 --> 00:01:14.974
I just think that I was born with a big head already.

00:01:14.975 --> 00:01:19.078
And you can actually see all the way from when I was about a year and a half

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:24.951
to about four or five years, all the way to just a few months before I left Mexico when

00:01:24.952 --> 00:01:31.424
I was about 19 years old, and I came to this beautiful country, the United States.

00:01:31.425 --> 00:01:36.596
I had an incredible trajectory in my life--wonderful role models.

00:01:36.597 --> 00:01:42.034
And it all began in my house, the role models that I had

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:46.672
as I was growing up with my parents, my siblings.

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:54.447
In 1971, my little sister died of diarrhea and a gastrointestinal infection.

00:01:54.448 --> 00:01:59.852
She was only 6 months old and it was a major event in my life,

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:03.890
and at a very young age, at only the age of 3,

00:02:03.891 --> 00:02:11.831
I began to realize that there is a difference between life and death.

00:02:11.832 --> 00:02:16.736
Such a young age and to think that these sorts of things could actually

00:02:16.737 --> 00:02:19.172
happen and still happen around the world.

00:02:19.173 --> 00:02:25.111
It makes me reflect very deeply into my role in this world.

00:02:25.112 --> 00:02:33.452
By 1979, my father told me that I was the architect of my own destiny.

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:38.124
I paid very close attention to all the things that

00:02:38.125 --> 00:02:41.861
my parents, my grandparents, my siblings told me.

00:02:41.862 --> 00:02:46.032
And right around that time I began to realize that indeed

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:54.540
we do have things in this world that we do for ourselves.

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:57.610
To some extent, we are the architects of our own destiny.

00:02:57.611 --> 00:03:07.186
Santiago Ramon y Cajal once said the chance in good luck does not come to those who want it.

00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:10.056
It comes to those who look for it.

00:03:10.057 --> 00:03:12.758
And I kept looking for my own good luck

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:16.796
all the way since I was that little boy that you saw with the big head.

00:03:16.797 --> 00:03:21.300
By 1987, Mexico had gone through a very difficult time.

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:26.572
The economy was scrambling and I decided to come to the United States.

00:03:26.573 --> 00:03:32.745
I immigrated to the United States as an illegal, undocumented alien to work

00:03:32.746 --> 00:03:36.015
in the San Joaquin Valley as a migrant farm worker.

00:03:36.016 --> 00:03:40.920
My parents saw their first son leaving the country.

00:03:40.921 --> 00:03:44.757
I have to put it in perspective because maybe about 15 years prior

00:03:44.758 --> 00:03:53.366
my mother had seen her own brother leave the country never to return and never to be found.

00:03:53.367 --> 00:03:58.804
This is the place between Mexico and the United States

00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:02.475
where I actually hopped the fence.

00:04:02.476 --> 00:04:05.545
We're looking at it from the American side,

00:04:05.546 --> 00:04:18.191
and this exact place where back in 1987 I looked and tried to find my own good luck.

00:04:18.192 --> 00:04:22.962
It reminds me of what Eleanor Roosevelt said once,

00:04:22.963 --> 00:04:29.268
"No one can make us feel inferior without our permission."

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:33.673
And those early days were difficult. I can't deny that.

00:04:33.674 --> 00:04:42.315
Those early days of living in this trailer, which, by the way, I paid $300 for it.

00:04:42.316 --> 00:04:48.254
And a lot of my friends now tell me that I was taken advantage of.

00:04:48.255 --> 00:04:51.490
Not only was this a small little house in the middle of nowhere,

00:04:51.491 --> 00:04:53.926
that I call my first mansion,

00:04:53.927 --> 00:04:57.964
but the reality is that the first night, when I was living in this mansion,

00:04:57.965 --> 00:05:04.871
it started to rain. And that night I woke up completely wet from water from everywhere,

00:05:04.872 --> 00:05:09.175
and I took a picture of this trailer as you can actually see right here,

00:05:09.176 --> 00:05:12.645
where I was actually able to fix the roof.

00:05:12.646 --> 00:05:18.417
Very early on, not only was I working in the fields of the San Joaquin Valley,

00:05:18.418 --> 00:05:28.027
with these very same hands, but I was also already, beginning to fix things that were not working.

00:05:28.028 --> 00:05:33.332
How little did I know that these very same hands were going to be one day

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:41.174
operating on brain tumors and doing science to try to advance the field of brain cancer.

00:05:41.175 --> 00:05:46.712
I drove some of the most sophisticated machines around that time.

00:05:46.713 --> 00:05:53.419
This is a particular cotton picker and as you can see I was so full of life, so full of energy.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:57.456
And this is actually a machine that always gets me excited to talk about it

00:05:57.457 --> 00:06:03.196
because right here in the inside of this machine, as you can see, you can actually sit,

00:06:03.197 --> 00:06:09.602
not much different than the way I sit today in my operating room chair.

00:06:09.603 --> 00:06:14.006
Just like an astronaut chair and you're controlling this big machine

00:06:14.007 --> 00:06:18.444
that is picking cotton with both of your hands and both of your feet.

00:06:18.445 --> 00:06:23.082
Very, very early on I was having the practice that was going to allow me

00:06:23.083 --> 00:06:28.387
one day to be able to do brain surgery completely connected to my patients.

00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:34.060
The only difference nowadays is that the microscope I also control it with my mouth.

00:06:34.061 --> 00:06:38.297
And I put a mouthpiece that I control and just like back then,

00:06:38.298 --> 00:06:42.502
I used to drive the machine, now I also control the microscope with my mouth

00:06:42.503 --> 00:06:47.507
and I can zoom in, zoom out, move the microscope from side to side

00:06:47.508 --> 00:06:49.675
and be able to navigate the brain.

00:06:49.676 --> 00:06:52.245
Back then I was navigating the cotton fields;

00:06:52.246 --> 00:06:55.381
now I've been privileged to be given the opportunity

00:06:55.382 --> 00:07:00.353
by my patients and their families to be able to navigate the brain.

00:07:00.354 --> 00:07:06.959
I was an entrepreneur. I took risks. Early on, I began my own trucking company.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.329
This is a truck that I bought and I had a dream that

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:13.466
one day I was going to have a whole fleet of trucks

00:07:13.467 --> 00:07:17.670
that was going to allow me to carry cotton from place to place

00:07:17.671 --> 00:07:20.840
some of the products in the San Joaquin Valley.

00:07:20.841 --> 00:07:27.013
So early on I was getting already the early teachings of taking risks.

00:07:27.014 --> 00:07:30.950
Unfortunately, things don't always work the way you want them.

00:07:30.951 --> 00:07:36.856
And as you can see, this truck, I actually ended up getting completely damaged

00:07:36.857 --> 00:07:42.295
not only because I myself couldn't actually fix the engine that eventually completely blew up

00:07:42.296 --> 00:07:47.200
because I didn't know much about mechanics, but I began to learn through reading a book.

00:07:47.201 --> 00:07:49.101
But the lessons that I learned back then

00:07:49.102 --> 00:07:55.007
are never to give up and always to try to push the field forward.

00:07:55.008 --> 00:07:59.011
And I pushed the field forward with this little truck back then

00:07:59.012 --> 00:08:01.814
by trying to fix it, by trying to fix the engine,

00:08:01.815 --> 00:08:07.620
by trying to fix the ability of this truck to work in the fields.

00:08:07.621 --> 00:08:12.225
Eventually, I actually ended up leaving the fields of the San Joaquin Valley.

00:08:12.226 --> 00:08:17.930
I was told once by one of my co-workers that I was going to spend the rest of my life

00:08:17.931 --> 00:08:20.833
working in the fields just like many generations of

00:08:20.834 --> 00:08:24.337
Mexican-Americans who had immigrated to the San Joaquin Valley.

00:08:24.338 --> 00:08:30.109
I was going on 20 years old, so full of life, so full of hope, so full of energy

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:34.714
and to be told this is not something that I was willing to accept.

00:08:34.715 --> 00:08:38.017
By the time I was in Berkeley I remember I was given a card

00:08:38.018 --> 00:08:42.588
by one of my classmates with a quote from Cesar Chavez that said

00:08:42.589 --> 00:08:47.527
"If you're not frightened that you might fail, you'll never do the job.

00:08:47.528 --> 00:08:51.998
If you are frightened, you will work like crazy."

00:08:51.999 --> 00:08:54.967
And "like crazy" I have worked my whole life.

00:08:54.968 --> 00:08:58.704
Not only as a young child working in the gas station of my parents,

00:08:58.705 --> 00:09:03.342
but now as an adult, now currently as a brain tumor surgeon

00:09:03.343 --> 00:09:06.345
and as a scientist trying to push the field.

00:09:06.346 --> 00:09:11.918
On April 14, 1989, just a couple of years before I went to UC Berkeley,

00:09:11.919 --> 00:09:16.789
I had a major event in my life in which I almost lost my life.

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:20.860
And I remember waking up and seeing my father.

00:09:20.861 --> 00:09:24.197
After everything was under control and I was out of danger, he told me

00:09:24.198 --> 00:09:33.105
that I was given a precious gift, that life was short, just to be good to others.

00:09:33.106 --> 00:09:38.311
This is an early picture of me in the middle with my brother, Gabriel

00:09:38.312 --> 00:09:44.350
and one of my coworkers in which I was working in this railroad company

00:09:44.351 --> 00:09:47.453
in which I almost left my life.

00:09:47.454 --> 00:09:56.829
However, I never gave up and I never, never was afraid of continuing to work really hard

00:09:56.830 --> 00:10:00.900
towards a dream—a dream that I was going to make it not only better for

00:10:00.901 --> 00:10:05.671
myself and my family, but potentially for future generations.

00:10:05.672 --> 00:10:10.643
I always tell my students that there is a fine line between being a mentor and being a tormentor,

00:10:10.644 --> 00:10:14.347
and I can personally tell you that I probably cross that line.

00:10:14.348 --> 00:10:16.215
My first mentor was Joe Martinez.

00:10:16.216 --> 00:10:21.053
This is a picture in 1993 in one of his laboratory meetings at his house.

00:10:21.054 --> 00:10:26.025
I was given the opportunity by Dr. Martinez to be part of science.

00:10:26.026 --> 00:10:34.300
And since then my life began. He was an influential figure in my life.

00:10:34.301 --> 00:10:39.038
This is 1994. By the time I graduated from UC Berkeley with Honors,

00:10:39.039 --> 00:10:45.144
I ended up following what my grandfather used to tell me as a young child,

00:10:45.145 --> 00:10:52.451
"Do not go where the path may lead, go instead where there is no path and leave a trail."

00:10:52.452 --> 00:10:58.157
And thanks to Dr. Martinez, I was inspired to then pursue my medical education

00:10:58.158 --> 00:11:03.663
at Harvard Medical School where I met two other great mentors,

00:11:03.664 --> 00:11:06.799
Dave Potter and Ed Kravitz.

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:11.304
Dr. Potter and Dr. Kravitz were not only role models, but were people

00:11:11.305 --> 00:11:15.408
who believed that I could actually make a difference in science.

00:11:15.409 --> 00:11:18.110
But, I never forgot where I came from.

00:11:18.111 --> 00:11:24.150
This is a picture that I did with two of my classmates, Miguel Canales and Ruben Fragoso,

00:11:24.151 --> 00:11:29.255
in which we have symbolisms of the Aztec culture by the sun,

00:11:29.256 --> 00:11:35.228
in which we are all moving upwards in our lives but turning back

00:11:35.229 --> 00:11:38.764
and trying to help others that are behind.

00:11:38.765 --> 00:11:44.403
This is the theory of La Escalerita, which is "the ladder,"

00:11:44.404 --> 00:11:46.906
the ladder to move upwards in our society.

00:11:46.907 --> 00:11:51.344
I strongly believe that education is the best provision for old age.

00:11:51.345 --> 00:11:54.780
I was told once, by the time I graduated from Harvard...

00:11:54.781 --> 00:11:58.451
Here I am at Harvard Medical School giving the commencement speech,

00:11:58.452 --> 00:12:04.257
chosen by my classmates, and I was reminded what Vince Lombardi once said,

00:12:04.258 --> 00:12:10.062
"The quality of a person's life is in direct proportion to their commitment to

00:12:10.063 --> 00:12:13.766
excellence, regardless of the chosen field of endeavor."

00:12:13.767 --> 00:12:19.405
Here I was, now as a resident, being doctored by my own daughter

00:12:19.406 --> 00:12:24.777
outside of the operating room and in the operating room using some of the most

00:12:24.778 --> 00:12:29.282
sophisticated equipment to be able to perform brain surgery.

00:12:29.283 --> 00:12:34.353
I pursue research, and you will learn more in my next segments,

00:12:34.354 --> 00:12:39.358
of brain tumors and stem cells, but I was able to work with

00:12:39.359 --> 00:12:45.198
astrocytes, stars that lie within the brain that we believe are stem cells.

00:12:45.199 --> 00:12:50.536
How little that I know that that little boy that worked in this little gas station at the age of 5

00:12:50.537 --> 00:12:55.274
was one day going to be surrounded by incredible stars.

00:12:55.275 --> 00:12:59.879
This is a picture, a few years ago, of the graduation of some of my residents

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:03.182
and to my right is Dr. Alessandro Olivi.

00:13:03.183 --> 00:13:10.356
Then if you look further, is Dr. Henry Brem and then right next to Dr. Henry Brem

00:13:10.357 --> 00:13:16.429
is one of the greatest icons of African-American medicine, which is Dr. Ben Carson.

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:18.898
I am being surrounded by stars.

00:13:18.899 --> 00:13:22.735
This is a picture of astrocytes that I use in my research.

00:13:22.736 --> 00:13:26.305
I get to do and use the most sophisticated equipment

00:13:26.306 --> 00:13:32.712
in the operating room because I believe that my role as a physician

00:13:32.713 --> 00:13:38.184
and surgeon and scientist is not only to give hope inside of the operating room,

00:13:38.185 --> 00:13:41.053
but also outside the operating room through our work.

00:13:41.054 --> 00:13:47.393
"The brain," I was told once, "is not a vessel to be filled, but a fire to be ignited."

00:13:47.394 --> 00:13:50.129
We have a high drop out rate.

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:54.167
By the year 2050 we're going to be one of the largest groups,

00:13:54.168 --> 00:13:57.870
Hispanic population, of this country.

00:13:57.871 --> 00:14:01.407
1 out of 4 people by 2050 will be of Hispanic descent.

00:14:01.408 --> 00:14:08.080
If we don't educate them we will not be able to continue to sustain this beautiful country.

00:14:08.081 --> 00:14:15.154
So I believe that through determination, dedication, discipline, resilience,

00:14:15.155 --> 00:14:22.328
excitement, admiration and mentorship, we can do great things together.

00:14:22.329 --> 00:14:25.431
"Success," Winston Churchill once said,

00:14:25.432 --> 00:14:29.502
"is going from failure to failure without the loss of enthusiasm."

00:14:29.503 --> 00:14:33.105
I tell my own children that sometimes we fall

00:14:33.106 --> 00:14:36.542
so we can have an opportunity to get up and try again.

00:14:36.543 --> 00:14:40.947
I tell my own children the lessons that I learned from my own grandfather,

00:14:40.948 --> 00:14:45.451
that a fool with a good tool is still a fool.

00:14:45.452 --> 00:14:49.455
That it is not the tool that matters, but what you do with it.

00:14:49.456 --> 00:14:52.758
And your education is a tool and the better you use it,

00:14:52.759 --> 00:14:58.831
the better we will be able to keep this country as beautiful as it is today.

00:14:58.832 --> 00:15:01.968
I am thankful for the people that have surrounded me.

00:15:01.969 --> 00:15:07.273
All these young people that keep me energized and vibrant and up-to-date,

00:15:07.274 --> 00:15:11.777
not only in science, but also in education, in medicine

00:15:11.778 --> 00:15:14.881
and by continuing to give hope to my patients.

00:15:14.882 --> 00:15:16.749
I thank you for your attention.

00:15:16.750 --> 00:15:19.283
Dr. Quiñones here from Johns Hopkins.

